<h2>Who decides when tickets go on sale?</h2>
<p>Event schedules and release timelines are decided by organizers, performers and promoters. They determine when tickets become available and how they are distributed to the public. Why are concert tickets so expensive right at the on-sale moment? Because the whole crowd arrives at once, and limited supply meets peak demand.</p>
<p>Automated buying is a known problem in ticketing, and our <a href="/terms-and-conditions">terms and conditions</a> prohibit bots and scrapers for bulk ticket purchasing.</p>

<h2>If concert tickets are so expensive, how can you pay less?</h2>
<ol>
<li>Compare several sections and rows for the same event.</li>
<li>Check the total with fees, not only the listed price.</li>
<li>Look at <a href="/ticket-deals">ticket deals</a> and try a <a href="/tickets-promo-code">promo code</a> if one is available.</li>
<li>Be flexible on date, or browse <a href="/concert-tickets-for-sale">concert tickets</a> for a different show.</li>
<li>Buy where you have protection: see <a href="/ticket-buyer-protection">ticket buyer protection</a> and the <a href="/worry-free-guarantee">Worry Free Guarantee</a>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Questions about delivery or entry? The <a href="/ticket-faq">ticket FAQ</a> has short answers.</p>

<h2>Questions about why concert tickets are so expensive</h2>
<details class="so-faq"><summary>Why are concert tickets so expensive for some shows and not others?</summary><p>Prices depend on demand, seat location, event popularity and availability, plus fees. Popular tours with few dates tend to cost the most.</p></details>
<details class="so-faq"><summary>Why are concert tickets so expensive before fees are even added?</summary><p>Event organizers, performers or promoters determine ticket pricing, and prices vary with demand, seat location, popularity and availability.</p></details>
<details class="so-faq"><summary>Why are concert tickets so expensive on resale and are they always higher?</summary><p>No. Sellers set resale prices, so they can be above or below face value.</p></details>
